WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.917
Loading FxRates takes 20 ms, so you build it on first use: if the field is null, build one.

2
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:11.537
Thread A asks first.

3
00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:13.923
It sees null, and starts building.

4
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:17.123
While A is still building, thread B asks.

5
00:00:17.123 --> 00:00:18.199
What does B see?

6
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:20.293
Null.

7
00:00:20.293 --> 00:00:22.767
A hasn't stored anything yet.

8
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:24.473
So B builds one too.

9
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.234
A stores its object.

10
00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:28.840
Then B stores another one over it.

11
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.757
Two threads, two FxRates.

12
00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:33.915
That's the same check-then-act race as count++.

13
00:00:33.915 --> 00:00:36.676
Eight threads arriving together build eight.

14
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:41.563
The fix: one thread checks and builds, and the others wait.

15
00:00:41.563 --> 00:00:45.525
The JVM already has a lock for that: class initialization.

16
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.804
Put the instance in a nested class, Holder.

17
00:00:48.804 --> 00:00:53.351
The JVM initializes Holder once, on first use, under that lock.

18
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:56.450
A gets there first and takes the lock.

19
00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.057
B waits.

20
00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:59.416
A builds the one FxRates.

21
00:00:59.416 --> 00:01:03.041
Then the lock opens, and B reads the same object.

22
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:06.943
Every later call is a plain static read, with no lock.

23
00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:11.893
If the object is cheap, use an enum with one constant.

24
00:01:11.893 --> 00:01:14.524
The JVM makes exactly one INSTANCE.

25
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.576
Kotlin's object compiles to a static INSTANCE, set when the class initializes: the holder's mechanism.

26
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.659
And Kotlin's by lazy takes a lock by default, and runs the initializer once.

27
00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:29.536
It's double-checked locking, written for you.

28
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:32.120
But look at the caller.

29
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.079
PaymentPricer fetches FxRates itself, inside a method.

30
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:39.700
A test wants a rate of 1.10, and can't reach in.

31
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.493
And a change one test makes to the global leaks into the next test.

32
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.928
So pass it in through the constructor, as an interface.

33
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:55.076
In production, the container makes the one instance, like an @ApplicationScoped bean in Quarkus.

34
00:01:55.076 --> 00:01:57.896
A test passes a fake that returns 1.10.

35
00:01:58.867 --> 00:01:59.525
The rule.

36
00:01:59.525 --> 00:02:06.475
Let the JVM or the container make the one instance, and pass it in, so a test can pass another.

37
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:07.607
Quick check.

38
00:02:07.607 --> 00:02:12.939
One test sets object FxRates to a rate of 1.10, and never resets it.

39
00:02:12.939 --> 00:02:16.623
A later test in another class prices a payment.

40
00:02:16.623 --> 00:02:18.427
Which rate does it get?

41
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:21.219
1.10.

42
00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:28.600
The object is one static INSTANCE per JVM, so the change outlives the test that made it.
